  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nSurface high pressure will drift through the Great Lakes today, with\ndry conditions for the ILN CWA, and high temperatures in the 70s.\n\nAs the high moves off to the east, a larger shift to the weather\npattern is expected to begin on Tuesday, with strong ridging (or \neven a closed high) setting up over the southeastern states. The push\nof warm and moist air from the south will bring temperatures into the\n80s to near 90 on Tuesday, Wednesday, and Thursday. Some heat index\nvalues near 100 may be possible in the southern parts of the forecast\narea on Wednesday.\n\nThe main frontal zone and storm track will be north of the ILN CWA to\nbegin on Tuesday, but this will shift southward with time as the\nridge breaks down. Recent model runs suggest this may happen a little\nsooner than previously expected, with chances for storms encroaching\non the northern ILN CWA as early as Tuesday night. Occasional rounds\nof showers and storms are then expected Wednesday, Thursday, and\nFriday, until the front finally moves south of the region late Friday\ninto the weekend.\n\nWith building instability, and some increase in wind flow working\ninto the area from north to south, some degree of severe threat may\nend up occurring during the week. However, with precipitable water\nvalues of 2\" or greater, and the potential for several rounds of\nstorms during the week, the flood threat may end up being the bigger\nconcern.",
